`IxMcp.Serve` is deleted: `Serve.app/2`, `Serve.status/1`, `Serve.stop/1`,
`Serve.State` and the alias in the workspace prelude. It served mkapp
scaffolds and nothing else, and mkapp is already gone.

It was never a general "serve a directory with a check-gated promote". It ran
`npm run check:staging` and `npm run promote`, polled a `staging/` tree for an
mtime signature, and preflighted with an error telling you to scaffold with
`mkapp` first. Every one of those was a template invention.

## Sequenced behind dashboard, not tidied up early

This did not land with the mkapp removal on purpose. A live report page was
still being served by `Serve` jobs inside a running kernel, and deleting the
source does not kill running jobs but the next kernel restart does. With
`Serve.app` gone and dashboard not yet able to carry a response, that would
have left a window with no working generative-UI surface at all.

`Dashboard.open("<id>")` now serves a document and returns a browser URL,
`Dashboard.html/4` and `Dashboard.data/5` publish panes into it, and
`Dashboard` is in the workspace prelude where `Serve` used to be. That is the
condition this change was waiting on.

## Also: a fifth no-op shape

`noOpChecks` in the house prompt gains one more thing to check for before
trusting a green: a wrapper reporting its own exit code instead of the
command's. A background `nix build ... > out 2>&1; echo "rc=$?" >> out` exited
0 while the build inside it exited 1, and the task notification read
"completed (exit code 0)". Only reading the file caught it. A sibling case the
same afternoon: a runner-liveness check whose 404 fires identically for a
healthy runner and a dead one.
